<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</h1><p class="st-meta">1436 CE – 1799 CE · capital Davos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The youngest of the Three Leagues of the Grisons, formed at Davos in 1436 when the last count of Toggenburg died without heirs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-vii-count-of-toggenburg">Frederick VII</a>, last count of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-toggenburg">County of Toggenburg</a>, died in 1436, the ten court districts (Gerichte) he had held in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prattigau">Prättigau</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/davos">Davos</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schanfigg">Schanfigg</a> formed a league at Davos to keep their rights and unity. Their lords remained, but the communes now acted together.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The league joined self-governing communes, each with its own court and assembly, and met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/davos">Davos</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> later acquired lordship over eight of the jurisdictions, causing friction until the communes bought out those rights between 1649 and 1652. The league allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-gods-house">League of God's House</a> in 1450 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grey-league">Grey League</a> in 1471, forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a>. Most communes became Reformed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The league ended with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Grisons</a> were attached to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> in 1799, and it became part of the canton of Graubünden in 1803.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions.jpg" alt="League of the Ten Jurisdictions" width="320" height="299"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (sidonius 15:49, 30 June 2006 (UTC)) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trois_ligues.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1436 CE – 1799 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Davos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Romansh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zehngerichtebund</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1618&amp;focus=league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions" aria-label="League of the Ten Jurisdictions on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4911 533 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where League of the Ten Jurisdictions lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5231 789L5224 788"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5224 788h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5231 789h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5231 789h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Davos. <a href="/atlas?year=1618&amp;focus=league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

